\section{A prototype small enough to falsify}

Build a bytecode VM with exact integers, Booleans, immutable arrays, deterministic traps and an observable \texttt{emit}. Exclude threads, external I/O and arbitrary edits from the initial guest language. Compare an independent reference evaluator against a slot-based baseline and one optimized loop compilation scheme. Use checked machine addition for the optimization, not wrapping arithmetic disguised as exact arithmetic. The build order in the dossier's system blueprint and implementation plan separates meaning, lowering, recovery, versioning and policy. Apply that order to the prototype: first make the baseline agree with the reference on traps and effects; next allow forced OSR and forced exits on demand; only then enable an adaptive policy (Dossier Chapters~02, 04 and~28).

Use one artifact record with semantic revision, entry sites, assumption IDs, code, OSR and deoptimization maps, and validation scope. Do not collapse the blueprint's exploration and assurance roles just because the same process provides both. The worker proposes a result; the installer rechecks its dependencies, code-size limit and recovery coverage. A candidate that never installs is still charged for its compilation CPU. Count code and recovery metadata against the footprint limit together.

\section{Exercise a full life cycle before timing it}

The loop below emits an index only after its element has been added successfully. Baseline and reference addition trap on Booleans. The fast version specializes to small exact integers. Its entry checks the current accumulator; its pre-add guard and checked add share a deopt continuation before addition and after loading the element. These are two distinct resume points: loop header for upward OSR, add instruction for deoptimization.

\begin{verbatim}
H: if i == len(a): return sum
L: x = a[i]
A: sum = exact_add(sum, x)
E: emit(i)
   i += 1
   goto H

test(a=[2, 4, true], initial_sum=0):
  run baseline through E for i=0
  at H with i=1, enter optimized loop
  complete i=1, fail small-integer check at A for i=2
  reconstruct {pc:A, i:2, sum:6, x:true, a:a}
  assert observations == [emit(0), emit(1), trap(TypeError)]
\end{verbatim}

Instrument a second assertion that the run actually entered native code and took the intended exit; an always-interpreted engine could otherwise pass. Run variants with maximum small integers, empty arrays, and misses on the first or final iteration. Reject one deliberately malformed snapshot and one result invalidated before installation, using an injected dependency change in the harness. Then run the same cases with adaptive policy disabled. These are acceptance tests, not evidence of a performance win. D'Elia and Demetrescu's \emph{On-Stack Replacement, Distilled} motivates checking live-state correspondence across versions; here the scope is one header and one exit, not their more general compensation framework (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-255}).

\Cref{fig:visual-260} shows why the test needs two different resume PCs: upward transfer enters before the loop test, while the guard exit resumes the addition with its operand already loaded.

