\section{Measure adaptation independently of code quality}

Titzer's \emph{Whose baseline compiler is it anyway?} studies compilation speed and runtime together across Wasm tiers. Izawa, Masuhara and Bolz-Tereick's \emph{Two-level Just-in-Time Compilation with One Interpreter and One Engine} additionally compares tiering choices and generated code size in adaptive RPython. To separate these questions in this prototype, hold the same optimized code generator fixed while testing interpreter-only, baseline-only, fixed backedge threshold, and adaptive threshold. Add an eager optimizer and a configuration with OSR disabled to isolate peak code quality and the benefit to a long-running frame, respectively. Dossier Chapters~33 and~41 propose budgeted allocation and adaptive granularity; evaluate changes to either one against the fixed policy before combining them.

Use equal input sequences and fresh processes for cold trials; pin target options and log run order and seeds. Include short runs that never amortize compilation, long type-stable loops, and a late phase of wider integers that repeatedly challenges the small-integer path. Record time to first result, total elapsed time, compiler CPU (including aborted work), queue delay, code plus metadata bytes, and exit and invalidation counts. For repeated requests report latency quantiles across independent runs with uncertainty, and plot completed work over time rather than discarding warm-up. Fix a success criterion before testing: for example, reduce elapsed time on the long and changing workloads without violating a cold-start tail-latency and footprint budget. Report a failed criterion even when peak throughput improves. The result is evidence about this implementation and workload mix, not a general endorsement of adaptive granularity or learned cost models (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-257}).

