\section{Use a Paired Acceptance Gate for Adaptive Policies}

A faster optimized loop is not enough to adopt a new controller.
Freeze one benchmark suite, per-workload inputs, and acceptance
criteria before tuning. For an illustrative gate, require a median
long-workload improvement of at least five percent, no higher
than ten percent regression in the cold-start tail, and a fixed
byte ceiling. Assume independent fresh-process pairs within each
workload and a predetermined number of pairs, alternating order.
For the long case use the median of paired relative reductions;
for cold start compare the two distributions' chosen quantile
estimates instead. Use the same generator and recovery maps under
both policies. An observation mismatch is a separate failure,
not an outlier to remove from timing.

\begin{sudocode}[A gate with correctness, benefit and ceilings]
\ForAll{preselected workload and seed pairs}
  \State run fixed and adaptive policies in balanced order
  \If{either observation sequence disagrees with the reference}
    \State \Return fail correctness \Comment{Do not score missing work}
  \EndIf
  \State record paired measurements and peak live bytes
\EndFor
\State $g\gets$ median paired relative reduction on long runs
\State $r\gets$ cold-start tail ratio, adaptive over fixed
\If{peak adaptive bytes exceed the ceiling}
  \State \Return fail footprint \Comment{Include pinned old versions}
\EndIf
\State \Return pass only if $g\geq 0.05$ and $r\leq 1.10$
\end{sudocode}

For three illustrative long pairs with fixed times $(100,120,110)$
ms and adaptive times $(90,114,99)$ ms, the paired reductions
are $(10,5,10)$ percent and the median is ten percent. If a
separate cold sample estimates the chosen tail at 20 ms fixed
and 23 ms adaptive, the ratio is 1.15 and the overall gate
fails. If the adaptive tail instead measures 21 ms but peak
footprint is 68 KiB against a 64 KiB ceiling, it still fails.

Every verdict uses equal observable work and the same
accounting convention. The numbers above explain decisions,
not statistical confidence. A deployment should collect enough
independent trials to estimate uncertainty, predeclare how to
handle an inconclusive interval, and rerun on workloads that
were not used to tune the controller. Keep the per-workload
results so a pooled headline cannot hide a regression.
